pbr-start-help = The first range where the bullet is inside the vital zone. 0 m means the sight is already inside the zone at the muzzle. That happens when the sight height is smaller than the zone radius.
pbr-near-zero-label = Near zero
pbr-near-zero-help = Where the bullet first rises through the line of sight. This is a zero crossing, not the start of the point-blank band.
pbr-far-zero-label = Far zero
pbr-far-zero-help = Where the bullet falls back through the line of sight. In optimal mode this is the zero range that puts the top of the arc on the zone.
pbr-end-label = Point-blank end
pbr-end-help = Where the bullet leaves the vital zone. With an optimal zero this is the descending exit through the bottom of the zone.
pbr-starts-inside = The sight height is smaller than the zone radius, so the point-blank band begins at the muzzle.
pbr-exits-above = With this zero the arc leaves through the top of the zone. The point-blank end shown is that exit.
pbr-ordinate-help = The highest the bullet climbs above the line of sight, and the range where that happens.
truing = Truing
truing-intro = Measure a group at a known range, type what you measured, and preview the fit. Preview only shows the change. Apply writes it onto this profile after you confirm. Muzzle-velocity truing changes the effective muzzle velocity used for every solve. Drag truing scales the ballistic coefficient, or the drag coefficient by Mach band if you ask for one scale per band. Tracking truing changes the click factor only. None of these rewrite the muzzle velocity stored on the load. A chronograph session is what changes that stored speed.
truing-kind = What to fit
truing-kind-tip = Choose what the measurement is allowed to change. Muzzle velocity changes the effective speed. Drag scales the ballistic coefficient. Tracking changes clicks only.
truing-lines-tip = Each line is one shot group: the range, the elevation hold that centred the group, how many shots, and the group size. The range and the hold are what you measured.
truing-piecewise-tip = Off: one ballistic-coefficient scale for every speed. On: a separate drag scale for the Mach band around each observation.
truing-advertised-tip = How far the scope maker says this many clicks should move the reticle. Type it as an angle, such as milliradians.
truing-observed-tip = How far the reticle actually moved when you counted those clicks, measured on a target. Tracking truing is the observed travel divided by the advertised travel, and it changes clicks only.
truing-range-tip = The distance at which you fired the group. The fit compares the solver with your measured hold at this range. The unit is the length unit on the button.
truing-elev-tip = The elevation hold that centred the group. This is the measured drop, written as an angle such as milliradians or minutes of angle, not as a length.
truing-shots-tip = How many shots were in the group. The number is stored with the truing entry. It does not change the fitted muzzle velocity.
truing-group-tip = The size of the group, for example 6 cm. It is stored with the truing entry. It does not change the fitted muzzle velocity.
truing-result-mv = This result changes the effective muzzle velocity on the profile. The muzzle velocity stored on the load stays as it was.
truing-result-drag = This result scales drag. One scale changes the ballistic coefficient. A scale per Mach band changes the drag coefficient in that band. The load's muzzle velocity stays as it was.
truing-result-tracking = This result changes the scope's click factor. Holds in milliradians stay the same, and the click count changes. Muzzle velocity and ballistic coefficient stay as they were.
